  {
   "type": "Intro",
   "props": {
    "id": "Intro-3",
    "band": "top",
    "blocks": [
     {
      "type": "Heading",
      "props": {
       "id": "Heading-1",
       "eyebrow": "For Planners",
       "slab": true,
       "title": "Run of show templates for a gala, a wedding and a holiday party.",
       "titleTag": "h1",
       "titleClass": "h2 display",
       "lede": "If you've ever put together a run of show, you know the band usually gets one row that says <em>band plays</em>, and the details get filled in by email the week of the event. We've worked those details out here for the three kinds of events we see most. You're welcome to use them, change the times and delete whatever doesn't apply."
      }
     }
    ],
    "_notes": "Run of show templates. The most useful thing on the site for a planner\n     who has not hired us: it is work they have to do anyway, done. The band\n     rows are the ones normally left as \"band plays\" until the week of.",
    "_file": "01-intro.html"
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "Text",
   "props": {
    "id": "Text-8",
    "band": "sunk",
    "blocks": [
     {
      "type": "Title",
      "props": {
       "id": "Title-4",
       "html": "How to read these templates",
       "tag": "h2",
       "cls": "h3"
      }
     },
     {
      "type": "Paragraph",
      "props": {
       "id": "Paragraph-5",
       "html": "The <strong>cue</strong> column is the most important one to get right. Someone needs to give the band the go-ahead for each moment of your event. What usually goes wrong is that two people each assume the other one will signal the band, or nobody knows who decides when the speeches are running long.",
       "cls": "prose"
      }
     },
     {
      "type": "Paragraph",
      "props": {
       "id": "Paragraph-6",
       "html": "We'll go over that column at sound check with whoever is holding the master document. Our bandleader takes their cues from that one person.",
       "cls": "prose"
      }
     }
    ],
    "_file": "01-intro.html"
   }
  },
